tton-wrapper\" data-attrs=\"{&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/subscribe?&quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Subscribe now&quot;,&quot;action&quot;:null,&quot;class&quot;:null}\" data-component-name=\"ButtonCreateButton\"><a class=\"button primary\" href=\"https://thefrontierpsychiatrists.substack.com/subscribe?\"><span>Subscribe now</span></a></p><p></p>","text":"I can’t hear a recording of Nolan without breaking down into tears on the street for a moment or two. And also, no one is immortal. I poist the contents of our grief often speaks volumes about our true feelings.\n\nNolan Ryan Williams, M.D., was the most important psychiatrist and scientist of my lifetime, and for reasons I sometimes find perplexing, he was very much part of my life. Nolan had a message, for us mortals, which was that psychiatric care could be better, faster, more effective, and here for those who need it. However, inventing a treatment and having easy access to a treatment are not the same thing—the workaround accessibility for everybody, at every moment of time, goes on. I wrote a eulogy at the time of his passing, and it’s available here.\n\nToday, the TED talk he recorded (about Ibogaine and it’s potential to heal brain injury and PTSD) is available to us all. This newsletter shares it :\n\nNolan Willliams' Ibogaine TED Talk\n\nMy prior article on Ibogaine is available:\n\nChris Aiken, M.D., another dear friend, has done some wonderful recent work explaining SAINT and it’s related tools in “other accelerated TMS” to the world, too:\n\nHe was loved, and his love and rigor will be saving lives for years to come.\n\nThank you, Nolan.\n\nFor those who didn't know him, he was a giant man—really physically large. He loved kite surf, he loved sweet tea, he loved the southern food from his native South Carolina, and loved his family, and he loved all of us, with all of his time, and more of his life force than anyone has spare. Thanks for reading.\n\nShare\n\nSubscribe now"}